    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 3's twelve-dots-two-per-string was the tidiest fact in this whole pathway, and it didn't survive. Sixteen to eighteen dots now, and only the C form manages three on every string. That's worth sitting with: the regular layer was the incomplete one. A box that's easy to memorise is a box that's missing something, and these windows are lumpy precisely because they're finished — that's what a complete major scale actually looks like, laid across six strings tuned the way a guitar is tuned."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [{ "text": "You Don't Memorise Seventeen Dots" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "You already know the roots, from chapter 1. You already know the chord tones, from chapter 2. Chapter 3 added the "
        },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and the " },
        { "text": "6", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". What's left is one rule, stated at the start of this chapter and true in every window with no exception: a "
        },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " sits one fret above every " },
        { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and a " },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " sits one fret below every " },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". Add those two facts to what you already had, and there's nothing left in any window that you have to learn as a new fact — only positions to find, using rules you already own."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "A " },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is one fret above every " },
        { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". A " },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is one fret below every " },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". True in all five windows, no exceptions — that's the whole memory aid." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "caged-ladder",
      "props": { "root": "C" }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "This is the same ladder chapter 1 ended on, and it still marks nothing but roots. That was too little to go on then. It is enough now. From any root you have a "
        },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " one fret below it, a " },
        { "text": "1 3 5", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " around it, and a whole scale after that. The ladder is the minimal map, not a tiling diagram — everything else regenerates from it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [{ "text": "Where All Six Strings Line Up" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Chapter 3 found fret " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " was the only fret where all six strings sound a pentatonic note. With the whole scale in, there are four such frets — "
        },
        { "text": "0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "10", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "12", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — and since " },
        { "text": "12", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is " },
        { "text": "0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " again an octave up, really three, five frets apart." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
