    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Look at what the other three carry that fret " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " doesn't. Fret " },
        { "text": "0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and fret " },
        { "text": "12", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " both hold a " },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the open " },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " on string 2. Fret " },
        { "text": "10", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " holds a " },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", at " },
        { "text": "3·10", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". Fret " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " is the one fret whose six notes dodge both of the new notes — which is exactly why it stood alone before this chapter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The reason is the same fact chapter 1 opened with: standard tuning is a stack of fourths with one major third at "
        },
        { "text": "G → B", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". Moving every string up five frets shifts each one exactly one step along that stack, and the whole set lands back inside the scale. It runs out after three jumps — fret "
        },
        { "text": "15", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " gives you " },
        { "text": "G C F A# D G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and " },
        { "text": "A#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " isn't a note of " },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " major, so the pattern breaks there." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "spans": [{ "text": "The Low E String, Completed" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Chapter 3 found the pentatonic on the low E string alone gives you frets " },
        { "text": "0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "8", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "10", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — exactly the five forms' barre frets. The scale adds two more: fret " },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and fret " },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". Below the twelfth fret, those are the only two scale notes on the low E string that aren't one of the five barre frets."
        }
      ]
